\section{Part 2: Techno-Economic Analysis of Biomass-to-Hydrogen Pathways}
\label{sec:tea}

Part~2 presents detailed techno-economic analysis demonstrating that modular biomass gasification offers technically credible and economically viable pathways for distributed hydrogen production. The analysis establishes cost competitiveness under realistic feedstock and energy price scenarios while revealing how clustering strategies and financing structures determine pathway viability.

\subsection{Analytical Framework and Viability Measures}
\label{subsec:viability-framework}

TEA viability is evaluated through net present value (NPV), internal rate of return (IRR), and levelized cost of hydrogen (LCOH, \$/kg). We customize operating-cost structures for three scales:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Single gasifier (50 kg/d H$_2$ capacity)
\item Medium cluster (10 units, 500 kg/d aggregate capacity)
\item Large cluster (100 units, 5,000 kg/d aggregate capacity)
\end{enumerate}

Analysis incorporates category-specific elasticities, shared services, and by-product credit utilization, applying broadly relevant U.S.\ inflation and interest rates across the project lifecycle (30-year horizon, 10\% discount rate).

\subsection{Technical Overview: Gasification Process and Scale Economics}
\label{subsec:technical-overview}

We evaluate two primary gasifier configurations:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Fixed-bed (FB):} Lower capital intensity, higher operating labor requirements
\item \textbf{Entrained-flow bed (EFB):} Higher capital intensity, lower operating labor requirements
\end{itemize}

Both produce green hydrogen from carbon-neutral biomass through thermochemical conversion. The process schematic (Figure~\ref{fig:process-schematic}) shows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Feed preparation:} Drying and pulverizing biomass feedstock
\item \textbf{Gasification:} Thermochemical conversion at elevated temperature and pressure
\item \textbf{Syngas cooling:} Temperature management for downstream processing
\item \textbf{Water-gas shift (WGS):} CO + H$_2$O $\rightarrow$ CO$_2$ + H$_2$
\item \textbf{Clean-up:} Particulate, tar, and contaminant removal
\item \textbf{Acid gas removal:} CO$_2$ and H$_2$S separation
\item \textbf{Pressure swing adsorption (PSA):} Final hydrogen purification
\item \textbf{By-product management:} Ash disposal, sulfur recovery
\end{enumerate}

