**Breadth-first search propagates rotations efficiently.** BFS ensures all gears at the same dependency depth update simultaneously, creating visual coherence. The BBFS-STT algorithm research shows average solving times under 1ms on mobile hardware for similar puzzles, proving this approach scales well:

```typescript
interface GearState {
  rotation: number;
  isAnimating: boolean;
}

const gearStates = new Map<string, GearState>();

function applyRotationBFS(startGear: string, angle: number): void {
  const queue: [string, number, string | null][] = [[startGear, angle, null]];
  const visited = new Set<string>();
  const updates: Array<{gear: string, angle: number}> = [];

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const [current, rotation, parent] = queue.shift()!;
    if (visited.has(current)) continue;

    visited.add(current);
    updates.push({ gear: current, angle: rotation });

    for (const neighbor of gearGraph[current]) {
      if (neighbor !== parent) {
        queue.push([neighbor, rotation, current]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Apply all updates simultaneously after propagation calculation
  updates.forEach(({gear, angle}) => {
    const state = gearStates.get(gear)!;
    state.rotation = normalizeAngle(state.rotation + angle);
    animateGearRotation(gear, angle);
  });
}
```

The key optimization: **calculate all updates before applying any**, ensuring dependent gears animate synchronously rather than sequentially cascading.

**Modular arithmetic handles angle normalization.** For 0-360Ã‚Â° range with always-positive results:

```typescript
function normalizeAngle(angle: number): number {
  return angle - Math.floor(angle / 360.0) * 360.0;
}
```

This avoids conditional branches, improving performance in tight loops. For calculating shortest angular distance (useful for hint systems showing proximity to solution):

```typescript
function shortestAngleDiff(from: number, to: number): number {
  let diff = (to - from + 180) % 360;
  if (diff < 0) diff += 360;
  return diff - 180;
}
```

**Win detection combines state checking with modular comparison.** Since each gear has a specific rotation amount, track total clicks rather than floating-point angles:

```typescript
