**Use integer-based state tracking** (click counts) rather than floating-point angles to eliminate precision errors. Check win condition by verifying `(clickCount * rotationAmount) % 360 === 0` for all three gears. This approach naturally handles the different rotation amounts (30Ã‚Â°, 45Ã‚Â°, 60Ã‚Â°) while maintaining perfect accuracy.

**Test solvability using group theory analysis.** With 12, 8, and 6 positions respectively, the large gear needs 12 clicks for full rotation, medium needs 8, small needs 6. The least common multiple (LCM) determines the full cycle: LCM(12, 8, 6) = 24 clicks returns the entire system to starting position. Calculate the greatest common divisor (GCD) of rotation amountsÃ¢â‚¬â€GCD(30Ã‚Â°, 45Ã‚Â°, 60Ã‚Â°) = 15Ã‚Â°Ã¢â‚¬â€which defines the finest achievable alignment precision.

**Add quality-of-life features** once core mechanics work: undo button (track state history), reset button, move counter, par count showing optimal solution length, optional timer for speedrunning, hint system showing proximity to solution (sum of angular distances from target).

**Implement accessibility from day one:** colorblind-safe palette with shapes on blade markers, high contrast mode, reduced motion option (instant rotation instead of animation), screen reader labels, keyboard controls (Tab to select gear, Space or Enter to rotate), adjustable UI scale.

**Performance optimization targets 60fps:** Batch DOM updates, use CSS transforms for performance (GPU-accelerated), avoid layout thrashing by reading all element positions before writing, debounce click events during animation, use requestAnimationFrame for any manual animation loops.

**Difficulty scaling strategies** for additional levels: Start positions closer to solution for early levels, increase scrambling for later levels, introduce move par requirements, add time limits for challenge mode, create levels with unique solutions vs. multiple solution paths, incorporate "bonus gears" that don't need alignment but affect others.

