\section{General Methodology}

Our framework consists of five integrated components that transform raw high-dimensional data into a computationally efficient predictive model while respecting physical conservation laws:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Input Consolidation:} Feature engineering to create normalized, congruent representations
\item \textbf{Geometric Transformation:} Dimensional reduction via canonical correlation analysis (CCA) and geometric projections
\item \textbf{Computational Laboratories:} Neural network units as statistical inference tools
\item \textbf{Characteristic Matrix Formation:} Eigenvalue-based signature of system state
\item \textbf{Special Function Parameterization:} Complete elliptic integrals via AGM for conservation-respecting output
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Input Consolidation: Construction of Representative Group}

The goal is to moderate extreme numerical fluctuations in parameters while preserving distinct event identities. This balances overall homogeneity with independence of individual cases.

\subsubsection{Equity Circle Normalization}

We start with a circle of unit area---analogous to a probability density function that integrates to unity:

$$\text{Area} = \pi r^2 = 1 \quad \Rightarrow \quad r = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}$$

This provides a common baseline for all data transformations, ensuring scale invariance.

\subsubsection{Sector Partitioning with Physical Meaning}

For biomass gasification, we partition the unit circle into four overlapping sectors representing:
\begin{itemize}
\item $s_1$: Steam-to-biomass ratio (SBR) influence
\item $s_2$: Temperature and equivalence ratio coupling
\item $s_3$: Biomass compositional characteristics (geometric features $d, K$)
\item $s_4$: Complementary interactions
\end{itemize}

The formulation with intentional overlap captures interaction effects:

\begin{align}
s_1 &= \frac{3 \cdot \text{SBR}}{100} + 0.05 \\
s_2 &= 0.7 \cdot \max\left\{\frac{T - 1000}{T}, \frac{T - 1000}{T} + \text{ER}\right\} \\
s_3 &= \frac{(1 + d^2)}{(1 + d^2 + 10^3 K^2/T)} \cdot (1 - (s_1 + s_2)) \\
s_4 &= 1 - (s_1 + s_2 + s_3)
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Two-Tiered Representation}

Following established practice, each sector is represented both by:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Population/Size:} Area of circular sector $A_i = s_i \cdot 1$
\item \textbf{Normalized Form:} Exactly one full quadrant per sector (independent of size)
\end{enumerate}

This dual representation preserves both magnitude and directional information, critical for subsequent matrix operations.

\subsubsection{Fibonacci Embedding}

To encode temporal or sequential structure, we embed data into Fibonacci sequences. For each data instance, we construct sequences:

\begin{align}
f_1 &= N_j + 2e^{-N_j} \\
f_2 &= e^{-N_j} \cdot f_2 \\
f_3 &= f_3 + f_2 \\
f_n &= f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \quad \text{for } n = 4, 5, \ldots, 11
\end{align}

where $N_j$ is a normalized composite parameter derived from geometric features, temperature, and sector ratios. The Fibonacci structure introduces hierarchical scale relationships that the neural network units will exploit.

