\section*{Appendix A: Detailed Derivations}

\subsection*{A.1 Geometric Transformations}

The transformation from four-dimensional CCA variates $\text{EPS} = [E_1, E_2, S_1, P_1]$ to two-dimensional geometric features $(d, K)$ proceeds as follows:

\textbf{Centroid calculations:}
\begin{align}
G_s &= \left( \frac{E_1 + E_2 + S_1}{3}, \frac{y_{E_1} + y_{E_2} + y_{S_1}}{3} \right) \\
G_p &= \left( \frac{E_1 + E_2 + P_1}{3}, \frac{y_{E_1} + y_{E_2} + y_{P_1}}{3} \right)
\end{align}

\textbf{Distance between centroids:}
$$d = \sqrt{(G_p^x - G_s^x)^2 + (G_p^y - G_s^y)^2}$$

\textbf{Midpoint and area:}
\begin{align}
M &= \left( \frac{G_p^x + G_s^x}{2}, \frac{G_p^y + G_s^y}{2} \right) \\
K &= \frac{1}{2} |E_1(y_{E_2} - y_M) + E_2(y_M - y_{E_1}) + M_x(y_{E_1} - y_{E_2})|
\end{align}

\subsection*{A.2 Fibonacci Sequence Generation}

Given normalized parameter $N_j$ derived from sector ratios, temperature, and geometric features:

\textbf{Initialization:}
\begin{align}
f_1 &= N_j + 2\exp(-N_j) \\
f_2 &= \exp(-N_j) \cdot f_2 \\
f_3 &= f_3 + f_2
\end{align}

\textbf{Recursion:}
$$f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \quad \text{for } n \geq 4$$

This generates sequences that encode system state in a hierarchically structured form, facilitating pattern recognition by neural networks.

\subsection*{A.3 Characteristic Matrix Outer Product}

Given score vectors $S_{AB} \in \mathbb{R}^9$ and $S_{BA} \in \mathbb{R}^9$ (from combining traces across 9 configurations):

\textbf{Normalization:}
$$\hat{S}_{AB} = \frac{S_{AB}}{\|S_{AB}\|}, \quad \hat{S}_{BA} = \frac{S_{BA}}{\|S_{BA}\|}$$

\textbf{Symmetric outer product:}
$$S_c = \frac{1}{2}\left( \hat{S}_{AB} \otimes \hat{S}_{BA}^T + \hat{S}_{BA} \otimes \hat{S}_{AB}^T \right)$$

This yields a $9 \times 9$ matrix reduced to $3 \times 3$ via reshaping, preserving essential structural information while compressing dimensionality.

\subsection*{A.4 AGM Convergence Rate}

The arithmetic-geometric mean converges quadratically. Starting from $a_0 = 1$, $b_0 = \sqrt{1-m}$:

\textbf{Error bound:}
$$|a_n - \text{AGM}(1, \sqrt{1-m})| \leq C \cdot 2^{-2^n}$$

for some constant $C$. This means:
\begin{itemize}
\item Iteration 1: $\sim$2 correct digits
\item Iteration 2: $\sim$4 correct digits
\item Iteration 3: $\sim$8 correct digits
\item Iteration 4: $\sim$16 correct digits (machine precision for double)
\end{itemize}

Thus $\sim$5 iterations suffice for all practical purposes.

