\section*{Abstract}
We present a revolutionary semi-analytical framework that bridges classical special function theory with modern data-driven modeling, offering a paradigm shift for high-dimensional systems governed by conservation laws. Originally developed and validated for biomass gasification in fluidized bed reactors---a complex multiphase reactive flow system---this methodology achieves mass and energy closure without explicit partial differential equation (PDE) solves through a novel integration of complete elliptic integrals computed via the arithmetic-geometric mean (AGM).

More than 200 datasets of biomass gasification featuring a wide range of operating conditions and fuel types were analyzed. The methodology employs an axiom-based reasoning framework to develop a multiphase statistical pathway that effectively quantifies the entanglements of critical process factors. By creating an interconnected chain of analysis based on trigonometric functions, geometric projections, and neural network computational laboratories, multiple partial measures of associations between biomass constituents and operating conditions are consolidated into a characteristic matrix. This matrix, combined with a novel ``specific computational time'' (sct) metric, feeds complete elliptic integrals of the first kind to achieve monotonic predictive relationships for carbon conversion efficiency and product gas yield across the full applicable range of influential parameters (temperature, air equivalent ratio, steam-to-biomass ratio, and fuel type).

\textbf{Methodological Classification:} This work occupies a distinct position on the analytical-numerical spectrum as a \emph{physics-informed, special-function-parameterized reduced-order model}. Unlike traditional numerical PDE solvers or purely data-driven approaches, it relocates computational expense from iterative PDE discretization to efficient special function evaluation ($\sim$5 AGM iterations to machine precision), while maintaining conservation constraints by construction through transfer function formulation.

\textbf{Broader Implications:} The framework's ability to handle high-dimensional data with conservation constraints while maintaining computational efficiency makes it particularly relevant for emerging trans-omics applications. Trans-omics research---pioneered by Japanese institutions for integrating genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics across temporal scales---faces similar computational challenges: high dimensionality, conservation constraints (mass, energy, entropy), need for fast inference, and integration of heterogeneous data types. While traditional biochemical modeling relies on Michaelis-Menten kinetics and rational function nonlinearities that rarely generate elliptic integrals naturally, our methodology demonstrates that special functions can serve as ``physics kernels'' for parameterized reduced-order models when dimensional reduction and conservation enforcement are paramount.

This technique holds transformative promise for accurately identifying patterns, revealing hidden relationships, and providing rapid insights for decision-making across energy systems, biochemical networks, and complex conservation-law-governed domains where traditional PDE approaches are prohibitive.

