\section{Application to Biomass Gasification}

\subsection{Research Objective}

Develop a general model for biomass gasification in fluidized beds predicting gasifier outputs ($\text{H}_2$, CO, $\text{CO}_2$, $\text{CH}_4$, carbon conversion efficiency, product gas yield) across wide ranges of:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Operating conditions:} Temperature ($T$), equivalence ratio (ER), steam-to-biomass ratio (SBR)
\item \textbf{Fuel types:} Woody, herbaceous, aquatic, waste-derived biomasses with varying:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Elemental composition (C, H, O, N, S)
    \item Proximate analysis (volatile matter, fixed carbon, ash)
    \item Structural components (cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin)
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Practical requirement:} Maintain computational time close to black-box models while incorporating physical constraints---enabling real-time optimization of fuel blends and operating conditions for power generation, hydrogen production, methanol synthesis, or Fischer-Tropsch hydrocarbons.

\subsection{Biomass Characterization Challenge}

Biomass characterization for gasification divides into three categories:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Well-characterized inputs:} Elemental composition (C, H, O, N, S)---extensively measured and directly used in most models

\item \textbf{Partially characterized inputs:} Proximate analysis (volatile matter, fixed carbon, ash) and structural components (cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin)---impacts recognized but less extensively investigated

\item \textbf{Unknown/highly variable features:} Ash composition, trace elements, structural heterogeneity---resulting in irreducible uncertainty about feedstock
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA)}

To integrate diverse biomass properties, we employ multi-stage CCA:

\subsubsection{Preconditioning Step}

Establish intrinsic connections within each property group by normalizing to the variable with smallest coefficient of variation:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Elemental group:} Normalize to Carbon
\item \textbf{Proximate group:} Normalize to Fixed Carbon  
\item \textbf{Structural group:} Normalize to Hemicellulose
\end{itemize}

Apply logarithmic transformation to ratios, ensuring direct internal correspondence.

\subsubsection{Cross-Group Integration}

Two distinct CCA analyses interconnect elemental, proximate, and structural groups---the minimum number needed to ensure all three are interlinked. This produces canonical variates:

$$\text{EPS} = [E_1, E_2, S_1, P_1]$$

representing maximal correlations across property classes.

\subsection{Geometric Transformation}

\subsubsection{Triangular Construction}

Reduce four-dimensional CCA output to two geometric features:

\textbf{Triangles:}
\begin{itemize}
\item $\Delta E_1 E_2 S_1$ with centroid $G_s$ (structural-elemental relationship)
\item $\Delta E_1 E_2 P_1$ with centroid $G_p$ (proximate-elemental relationship)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Derived features:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Distance} $d$: Between centroids $G_p$ and $G_s$---assesses how structural and proximate components position relative to elemental composition

\item \textbf{Area} $K$: Triangle formed by base $E_1 E_2$ and midpoint of $G_p$-$G_s$ line segment:
$$K = \frac{1}{2} |x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2)|$$
\end{enumerate}

These two parameters $(d, K)$ capture essential biomass variability in a computationally tractable form.

\subsection{Representative Group Construction}

Partition unit circle into four overlapping sectors reflecting:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Overall biomass characteristics (via $d$, $K$)
\item Temperature ($T$)
\item Equivalence ratio (ER)
\item Steam-to-biomass ratio (SBR)
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Design principles:}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Significance hierarchy:} Temperature appears in multiple sectors reflecting its dominant role
\item \textbf{Bounded variation:} Upper/lower limits prevent any sector from dominating
\item \textbf{Controlled randomness:} Fortify against high uncertainty by introducing randomness early
\item \textbf{Inclusiveness structure:} Internal hierarchy based on scope of influence
\end{itemize}

The formulation (see Section 3.1.2) ensures:
\begin{itemize}
\item Geometric uniformity via perpendicular dividing lines
\item Two-tiered representation (size and normalized form)
\item Strong correlation (72\%) between input and output geometric ratios
\end{itemize}

