\item[\textcolor{green}{$\checkmark$}] Handles high dimensionality
\item[\textcolor{green}{$\checkmark$}] Fast training and inference
\item[\textcolor{red}{$\times$}] No conservation guarantee
\item[\textcolor{red}{$\times$}] Black box (uninterpretable)
\item[\textcolor{red}{$\times$}] Poor extrapolation
\item[\textcolor{red}{$\times$}] Requires massive training data
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{The Inverse Problem Bottleneck}

Most computational effort in trans-omics goes to:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Parameter inference:} What are the reaction rates?
\item \textbf{Network identification:} Which molecules interact?
\item \textbf{Data integration:} How do we combine heterogeneous measurements?
\item \textbf{Uncertainty quantification:} How confident are our predictions?
\end{itemize}

Even if all forward ODEs had analytical solutions, these inverse/inference problems would remain computationally intensive.

\subsection{The Special-Function-Parameterized Approach}

Our methodology offers a third way that addresses both challenges:

\subsubsection{Dimensional Reduction via Feature Engineering}

