\subsubsection{Biochemical Network Structure}

Unlike energy systems where elliptic integrals arise from classical mechanics, biochemical networks produce rational function nonlinearities (Michaelis-Menten, Hill equations). Key research questions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Q1:} Can elliptic integrals effectively parameterize systems with rational nonlinearities?
\item \textbf{A1:} Our work demonstrates yes---the special functions serve as \emph{efficient basis functions} for learned reduced-order models, not as direct solutions to governing equations.

\item \textbf{Q2:} Does the AGM converge appropriately for biochemical parameter ranges?
\item \textbf{A2:} Preliminary tests suggest convergence is robust. The modulus $m \in [0, 1]$ maps naturally to normalized concentrations and ratios.

\item \textbf{Q3:} How do we interpret poles/zeros biologically?
\item \textbf{A3:} Poles represent characteristic time scales (protein lifetimes, reaction rates); zeros represent inhibition/cancellation effects (competitive binding, feedback loops).
\end{itemize}

