\subsection{Canonical Correlation Analysis for Multi-Group Integration}

\subsubsection{Motivation: Heterogeneous Biomass Characterization}

Biomass feedstock characterization divides into three distinct groups, each offering complementary but incommensurate perspectives:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Elemental composition:} Carbon (C), hydrogen (H), oxygen (O), nitrogen (N), sulfur (S)---extensively measured, directly relevant to stoichiometry, but lacking structural information

\item \textbf{Proximate analysis:} Volatile matter (VM), fixed carbon (FC), ash (A), moisture (M)---captures thermal decomposition behavior but obscures molecular structure

\item \textbf{Structural components:} Cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin---mechanistic insight into pyrolysis pathways but measurement-intensive and less commonly reported
\end{enumerate}

These groups exhibit internal correlations (e.g., high C correlates with high FC) and cross-group relationships (e.g., lignin content affects elemental O/C ratio). However, direct concatenation into a single feature vector is problematic:
\begin{itemize}
\item Different units and scales (mass\%, mol\%) create numerical ill-conditioning
\item Redundancy inflates dimensionality without adding information
\item Physical meaning obscures when disparate quantities are treated uniformly
\end{itemize}

\textbf{CCA addresses these challenges by identifying maximal correlations between groups while preserving within-group structure.}

\subsubsection{CCA Formulation}

Given two multivariate sets measured on the same samples:
$$\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_p], \quad \mathbf{Y} = [Y_1, Y_2, \ldots, Y_q]$$

CCA seeks linear combinations:
\begin{align}
U_1 &= a_{11}X_1 + a_{12}X_2 + \cdots + a_{1p}X_p \\
V_1 &= b_{11}Y_1 + b_{12}Y_2 + \cdots + b_{1q}Y_q
\end{align}

that maximize the canonical correlation $\rho_1^* = \text{Corr}(U_1, V_1)$ subject to $\text{Var}(U_1) = \text{Var}(V_1) = 1$. Subsequent pairs $(U_2, V_2)$, $(U_3, V_3)$, $\ldots$ are found iteratively, each uncorrelated with previous pairs, extracting decreasing magnitudes of shared variance.

\subsubsection{Multi-Stage CCA for Biomass Integration}

We employ a two-stage CCA architecture ensuring all three property groups (elemental, proximate, structural) are interconnected---the minimum graph configuration for full connectivity:

\textbf{Stage 1: Elemental--Proximate CCA}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Preconditioning:} Normalize elemental ratios to carbon: $\ln[\ln(C/O)]$, $\ln[\ln(C/H)]$, $\ln[\ln(C/N)]$, $\ln[\ln(C/S)]$
\item \textbf{Preconditioning:} Normalize proximate ratios to fixed carbon: $\ln[\ln(FC/VM)]$, $\ln[\ln(FC/A)]$
\item \textbf{CCA solution:} Yields canonical variates $(E_1, P_1)$ capturing maximal elemental--proximate correlation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Stage 2: Elemental--Structural CCA}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Preconditioning:} Normalize structural ratios to hemicellulose: $\ln[\ln(\text{Hem}/\text{Cel})]$, $\ln[\ln(\text{Hem}/\text{Lig})]$
\item \textbf{CCA solution:} Yields canonical variates $(E_2, S_1)$ capturing maximal elemental--structural correlation
\end{itemize}

The doubly-logarithmic transformation $\ln[\ln(\cdot)]$ serves two purposes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Normalizes skewed distributions (biomass ratios span orders of magnitude)
\item Creates group isomorphisms enabling direct correspondence between heterogeneous quantities
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Output:} Four canonical variates forming the \textbf{compact set}:
$$\text{EPS} = [E_1, E_2, S_1, P_1]$$

This four-dimensional representation captures essential biomass variability while integrating elemental, proximate, and structural information in a statistically principled manner.

\subsection{Geometric Transformation: Unlocking ``Open-Water'' Analysis Space}

\subsubsection{From Cartesian Constraints to Geometric Invariants}

Composition data---whether mole fractions, mass fractions, or probabilities---obey sum-to-one constraints (Dalton's law):
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} y_i = 1$$

In Cartesian coordinates, this constraint defines an $(n-1)$-dimensional hyperplane (simplex) embedded in $\mathbb{R}^n$. While mathematically valid, this representation suffers:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Brittle boundaries:} Constraint boundaries create numerical ill-conditioning (near-singular Jacobians in optimization)
\item \textbf{Limited geometric toolkit:} Euclidean distances on simplices lack physical interpretability
\item \textbf{Collinearity issues:} Variables are inherently dependent, inflating condition numbers
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Trigonometric parameterization} offers an elegant alternative. The Pythagorean identity:
$$\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1$$

is structurally identical to a two-component sum-to-one constraint. Mapping:
$$y_1 = \sin^2\theta, \quad y_2 = \cos^2\theta$$

embeds the 1-simplex ($y_1, y_2 \geq 0$; $y_1 + y_2 = 1$) onto a unit circle parameterized by $\theta \in [0, 2\pi]$. Generalizations to higher dimensions use spherical coordinates or combinations thereof.

\subsubsection{The ``Open-Water'' Metaphor}

We term this transformed space \textbf{``open water''} to emphasize liberation from brittle axis-aligned constraints. Traditional Cartesian treatment of compositions imposes artificial boundaries---the simplex edges and vertices---where numerical methods struggle (zero-valued components, gradient singularities). Trigonometric parameterization removes these boundaries:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Constraints become intrinsic topology:} The unit circle/sphere geometry naturally enforces normalization without explicit constraints
\item \textbf{Smooth, periodic parameterization:} Angular coordinates are differentiable everywhere; no special treatment for boundary cases
\item \textbf{Geometric features are noise-tolerant:} Triangle centroids, side lengths, areas computed from angular projections exhibit superior numerical conditioning
\item \textbf{Physical interpretability preserves:} Geometric distances approximate compositional dissimilarity in a metrically meaningful way
\end{itemize}

This ``open water''---absence of brittle axis-aligned constraints---provides a larger toolkit of identities and geometric invariants that still respect first principles. It is the foundation for stable feature extraction feeding subsequent elliptic integral parameterization.

\subsubsection{Triangular Construction from CCA Variates}

The four-dimensional CCA output EPS $= [E_1, E_2, S_1, P_1]$ is reduced to two geometric features via triangular projections:

\textbf{Triangle 1:} $\Delta E_1 E_2 S_1$ with centroid $G_s$ (structural--elemental relationship)
\textbf{Triangle 2:} $\Delta E_1 E_2 P_1$ with centroid $G_p$ (proximate--elemental relationship)

\textbf{Centroid calculations:}
\begin{align}
G_s &= \left( \frac{E_1 + E_2 + S_1}{3}, \frac{y_{E_1} + y_{E_2} + y_{S_1}}{3} \right) \\
G_p &= \left( \frac{E_1 + E_2 + P_1}{3}, \frac{y_{E_1} + y_{E_2} + y_{P_1}}{3} \right)
\end{align}

\textbf{Geometric features:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Distance} $d$: Euclidean distance between centroids $G_p$ and $G_s$
$$d = \sqrt{(G_p^x - G_s^x)^2 + (G_p^y - G_s^y)^2}$$

This assesses how structural and proximate components position relative to elemental composition---a measure of multi-scale compositional heterogeneity.

\item \textbf{Area} $K$: Triangle formed by base $E_1 E_2$ and midpoint $M$ of $G_p$--$G_s$ line segment:
\begin{align}
M &= \left( \frac{G_p^x + G_s^x}{2}, \frac{G_p^y + G_s^y}{2} \right) \\
K &= \frac{1}{2} |E_1(y_{E_2} - y_M) + E_2(y_M - y_{E_1}) + M_x(y_{E_1} - y_{E_2})|
\end{align}

Area $K$ captures the ``span'' of biomass variability across property groups---larger $K$ indicates greater inter-group disparity.
\end{enumerate}

These two parameters $(d, K)$ distill 13 original biomass properties (5 elemental + 4 proximate + 3 structural + moisture) into a geometrically meaningful, numerically stable pair. Critically, $(d, K)$ are \emph{invariants} under common transformations (translation, scaling), providing robust features for downstream modeling.

\subsection{Hierarchical Sequence Encoding via Fibonacci Structure}

\subsubsection{Motivation: Multi-Scale Temporal Embedding}

Physical systems exhibit dynamics across multiple temporal scales. For gasification: particle heating (seconds), pyrolysis (seconds--minutes), char gasification (minutes--hours), reactor residence time (minutes). Capturing this multi-scale structure requires representations encoding hierarchical relationships.

\textbf{Fibonacci sequences}---where each term is the sum of the two preceding terms---naturally embed hierarchical, self-similar structure. While originally a discrete recurrence, Fibonacci-like growth appears in physical systems exhibiting exponential growth modulated by memory effects (previous states constrain future evolution).

\subsubsection{Sector-Based Parameterization}

For biomass gasification, we partition a unit-area circle (analogous to a probability density function integrating to unity: $\pi r^2 = 1 \Rightarrow r = 1/\sqrt{\pi}$) into four overlapping sectors representing process drivers:

\begin{itemize}
\item $s_1$: Steam-to-biomass ratio (SBR) influence
\item $s_2$: Temperature (T) and equivalence ratio (ER) coupling
\item $s_3$: Biomass compositional characteristics (geometric features $d, K$)
\item $s_4$: Complementary interactions (cross-coupling terms)
\end{itemize}

Sector areas encode relative importance of each factor, normalized to sum to unity. A composite variable $N_j$ is constructed incorporating:
\begin{itemize}
\item Sector ratios (e.g., $s_1/s_2$, $s_3/s_4$)
\item Operating conditions (T, ER, SBR)
\item Geometric features ($d$, $K$)
\item Physical constants (gas constant $R$, Faraday constant $F$) providing dimensionality linking
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Fibonacci Sequence Generation}

Given normalized parameter $N_j$ derived from the above sector construction:

\textbf{Initialization:}
\begin{align}
f_1 &= N_j + 2\exp(-N_j) \\
f_2 &= \exp(-N_j) \cdot f_1 \\
f_3 &= f_1 + f_2
\end{align}

\textbf{Recursion:}
$$f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \quad \text{for } n \geq 4$$

This generates sequences encoding system state in a hierarchically structured form. Exponential damping in initialization prevents unbounded growth while preserving self-similar structure. The sequences:
\begin{itemize}
\item Capture both local (recent terms) and global (cumulative sum) information
\item Exhibit scaling properties facilitating pattern recognition by neural networks
\item Provide dimensionless representations enabling cross-case comparisons
\end{itemize}

Typically, sequences of length 10--15 suffice to capture essential dynamics without computational burden.

\subsection{Neural Network Computational Laboratories}

\subsubsection{Architecture: Three Activation Function Laboratories}

Rather than training a single large neural network, we employ three small networks---each a ``computational laboratory''---with different activation functions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Linear (L):} $\phi(z) = z$---captures linear relationships, gradient information
\item \textbf{Hyperbolic tangent (T):} $\phi(z) = \tanh(z) = (e^z - e^{-z})/(e^z + e^{-z})$---captures smooth nonlinearities, bounded output
\item \textbf{Exponential/Sigmoid (E):} $\phi(z) = 1/(1 + e^{-z})$---captures saturation, asymmetry
\end{enumerate}

Each network receives Fibonacci sequences as input and produces a $5 \times 5$ matrix of internal weights/biases after training on a subset of gasification data. The networks are \emph{not} used for direct prediction; instead, they serve as \textbf{statistical inference tools} extracting structure from sequences.

\subsubsection{Trace Analysis and Characteristic Scoring}

For each trained network and each data case, we compute:
$$\text{tr}(M) = \sum_{i=1}^{5} M_{ii}$$

where $M$ is the $5 \times 5$ internal matrix. The trace---sum of eigenvalues---is an invariant under similarity transformations, encoding aggregate system behavior without sensitivity to basis choice.

Running Fibonacci sequences from all 200+ cases through all three networks (L, T, E) in both forward (A$\to$B) and reverse (B$\to$A) directions generates:
$$3 \text{ networks} \times 2 \text{ directions} \times 3 \text{ configurations} = 18 \text{ traces per case}$$

These traces are consolidated into two score vectors:
\begin{align}
S_{AB} &\in \mathbb{R}^9 \quad \text{(forward-direction scores)} \\
S_{BA} &\in \mathbb{R}^9 \quad \text{(reverse-direction scores)}
\end{align}

The asymmetry between forward and reverse captures \textbf{path dependence}---gasification is an irreversible thermodynamic process, and this directional asymmetry encodes hysteresis, rate limitations, and non-equilibrium effects.

\subsection{Characteristic Matrix Formation}

\subsubsection{Outer Product Construction}

Given score vectors $S_{AB}$ and $S_{BA}$, we first normalize to unit length:
$$\hat{S}_{AB} = \frac{S_{AB}}{\|S_{AB}\|}, \quad \hat{S}_{BA} = \frac{S_{BA}}{\|S_{BA}\|}$$

Then form the \textbf{symmetric outer product}:
$$S_c = \frac{1}{2}\left( \hat{S}_{AB} \otimes \hat{S}_{BA}^T + \hat{S}_{BA} \otimes \hat{S}_{AB}^T \right)$$

This yields a $9 \times 9$ matrix, which is reshaped (vectorized and refolded) into a $3 \times 3$ matrix preserving essential structural information while compressing dimensionality.

In Dirac notation, this operation can be written:
$$|S_c\rangle = |S_{AB}\rangle \otimes |S_{BA}\rangle$$

emphasizing the quantum-mechanical flavor of this construction---the characteristic matrix as a tensor product state capturing correlations between forward and reverse processes.

\subsubsection{Universal Structural Pattern}

Remarkably, across all 200+ cases spanning diverse fuels, reactor types, and operating conditions, the characteristic matrix $S_c$ exhibits a \textbf{consistent directional pattern}. While numerical values vary, the sign structure and dominant eigenvector orientation remain invariant---analogous to ferromagnetic ordering where local spins align to a global field.

This universality suggests $S_c$ captures fundamental thermodynamic topology independent of specific parameter values, encoding the constraint manifold geometry onto which all gasification trajectories are confined by conservation laws.

\subsection{Specific Computational Time (sct): A Novel Dynamic Property}

\subsubsection{Motivation: Beyond Static Equilibrium}

Traditional thermodynamic properties (temperature, pressure, composition) characterize static equilibrium states. However, gasification kinetics---char reactivity, tar cracking rates, transport resistances---govern \emph{approach} to equilibrium. How do we quantify non-equilibrium dynamics without solving time-dependent PDEs?

We introduce \textbf{specific computational time} (sct) as a proxy for ``computational rigidity''---the resistance a system configuration presents to algorithmic manipulation.

\subsubsection{Abstract Mechanical Operation}

Consider the $3 \times 3$ characteristic matrix $S_c$. Define an ``abstract mechanical operation'':

\begin{enumerate}
\item Transform matrix $\to$ vector: Vectorize $S_c$ to $9 \times 1$ column
\item Sort ascending order: Rearrange elements from smallest to largest
\item Measure duration: Record elapsed time for this operation
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Physical Analogy:} This mimics material deformation under stress. ``Stiff'' materials (high Young's modulus) resist deformation; similarly, certain matrix configurations may exhibit computational ``stiffness''---taking longer to sort due to memory access patterns, cache misses, or algorithmic branch mispredictions.

While this may seem abstract, the key insight is that \textbf{temporal disparities in computational operations correlate with physical system complexity}. Cases requiring more intricate chemical pathways or exhibiting greater kinetic limitations manifest as matrices requiring longer sorting times.

\subsubsection{Measurement and Interpretation}

In MATLAB, sct is measured via:
\begin{verbatim}
tic;
S_vector = reshape(S_c, [9, 1]);
S_sorted = sort(S_vector, 'ascend');
sct = toc;
\end{verbatim}

Typical values: sct $\sim$ 10$^{-5}$--10$^{-3}$ seconds. While tiny in absolute terms, \emph{relative} variations (order of magnitude across cases) encode meaningful information about system non-equilibrium character.

Importantly, sct is \textbf{non-equilibrium}---it does not exist in equilibrium thermodynamics but emerges from the algorithmic process of state characterization. This bridges the gap between static thermodynamic potentials and kinetic rate constants, providing a computationally-derived dynamic property without requiring time-series data.

\subsection{Complete Elliptic Integral Parameterization}

\subsubsection{Argument Construction}

Three quantities feed elliptic integral evaluation:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{sct}: Specific computational time (non-equilibrium factor)
\item \textbf{$\sum f_n$}: Cumulative sum of Fibonacci sequence (multi-scale embedding)
\item \textbf{T}: Operating temperature (thermodynamic driving force)
\end{enumerate}

These are combined via a learned nonlinear mapping $m = \mathcal{F}(\text{sct}, \sum f_n, T)$ to produce the elliptic integral parameter $m \in [0, 1)$. The specific functional form $\mathcal{F}$ is determined via regression on training data, subject to the constraint that $m$ remains within the convergent domain of $K(m)$.

\subsubsection{Transfer Function Generation via AGM}

Given parameter $m$, the complete elliptic integral of the first kind is computed via AGM:

\textbf{Initialization:}
$$a_0 = 1, \quad b_0 = \sqrt{1-m}$$

\textbf{Iteration:}
\begin{align}
a_{n+1} &= \frac{a_n + b_n}{2} \quad \text{(arithmetic mean)} \\
b_{n+1} &= \sqrt{a_n b_n} \quad \text{(geometric mean)}
\end{align}

\textbf{Convergence:}
$$\lim_{n\to\infty} a_n = \lim_{n\to\infty} b_n = \text{AGM}(1, \sqrt{1-m})$$

\textbf{Elliptic integral:}
$$K(m) = \frac{\pi}{2 \cdot \text{AGM}(1, \sqrt{1-m})}$$

Error decays as $\sim 2^{-2^n}$ (quadratic convergence), yielding machine precision in $\sim$5 iterations.

\subsubsection{Pole-Zero-Gain Extraction}

The computed $K(m)$---along with related special function values---is mapped to transfer function parameters via an ``ellipap'' module (analogous to ellipap in MATLAB's Control System Toolbox):

$$H(s) = k \frac{\prod_{i=1}^{n_z} (s - z_i)}{\prod_{j=1}^{n_p} (s - p_j)}$$

where:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Zeros} $z_i$: Roots of numerator (frequencies at which output is zero)
\item \textbf{Poles} $p_j$: Roots of denominator (system resonances; must be in left half-plane for stability)
\item \textbf{Gain} $k$: Amplitude scaling factor
\end{itemize}

The ellipap module enforces:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Passivity:} $\text{Re}(p_j) < 0$ for all poles (stable system)
\item \textbf{Conservation:} Pole-zero topology and gain magnitude consistent with mass/energy balance
