\section{Application to Biomass Gasification}

\subsection{Dataset Description}

\subsubsection{Data Collection and Organization}

We compiled 200+ experimental datasets from 15 independent literature sources spanning two decades of gasification research. Data were systematically extracted, verified for consistency, and organized into four categories:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Biomass properties:} Elemental composition (C, H, O, N, S), proximate analysis (VM, FC, ash, moisture), structural components (cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin) for 38 distinct fuel types
\item \textbf{Operating conditions:} Temperature (600--900$^\circ$C), equivalence ratio (0.2--0.5), steam-to-biomass ratio (0--1.5), gasifying agent composition (air, oxygen, steam, mixtures)
\item \textbf{Reactor configuration:} Bubbling fluidized bed (BFB) and circulating fluidized bed (CFB); bed material, particle size distribution, superficial velocity
\item \textbf{Experimental observations:} Product gas composition ($\text{H}_2$, CO, $\text{CO}_2$, $\text{CH}_4$, $\text{N}_2$ on dry, nitrogen-free basis), total gas yield (Nm$^3$/kg biomass daf), carbon conversion efficiency (CCE\%)
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Data Quality Assurance}

Gasification literature exhibits significant variability in reporting standards. We implemented rigorous quality assurance:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Mass balance closure:} Elemental carbon, hydrogen, oxygen balances verified for consistency between input and output
\item \textbf{Nitrogen balance:} Where gas composition and inlet nitrogen were reported independently, cross-validation ensured consistency
\item \textbf{CCE calculation:} When only partial data were available (e.g., yield and composition but not CCE), missing values were calculated assuming 100\% carbon closure; unreasonable values ($<$70\% or $>$100\%) flagged for exclusion
\item \textbf{Outlier detection:} Cases with measurement inconsistencies (e.g., reported yield incompatible with composition and stoichiometry) were systematically identified and excluded
\end{itemize}

After quality filtering, 198 high-confidence datasets remained for model development and validation.

\subsubsection{Train-Test Split Strategy}

To ensure robust validation, we employed a conservative split strategy:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Training:} 168 datasets for CCA coefficient determination, neural network training, and elliptic integral parameter regression
\item \textbf{Validation:} 30 datasets held out during all training, used only for hyperparameter tuning and method selection
\item \textbf{Testing:} Independent datasets reported in comparative studies (Moshtaghi et al.) not used anywhere in development
\end{itemize}

Additionally, for yield and CCE predictions:
\begin{itemize}
\item 114 experimental cases with reported yield (previously unused for composition modeling)
\item 96 experimental cases with reported CCE (similarly independent)
\end{itemize}

This multi-tier validation strategy guards against overfitting and ensures reported performance metrics reflect true generalization capability.

\subsection{Implementation Details}

\subsubsection{CCA Execution}

Multi-stage CCA was implemented in MATLAB using the XLSTAT toolbox. For the 38 biomass types with complete elemental, proximate, and structural characterization:

\textbf{Elemental--Proximate CCA:}
\begin{align}
E_1 &= C^{0.167} O^{-0.167} H^{0.011} N^{0.635} S^{0.651} \\
P_1 &= \text{FC}^{0.521} \text{VM}^{-0.382} \text{A}^{0.903}
\end{align}

\textbf{Elemental--Structural CCA:}
\begin{align}
E_2 &= C^{0.193} O^{-0.193} H^{0.027} N^{0.581} S^{0.688} \\
S_1 &= \text{Hem}^{0.447} \text{Cel}^{-0.312} \text{Lig}^{0.765}
\end{align}

These canonical variates capture $>$85\% of variance between groups, enabling effective dimensional reduction while preserving cross-group relationships.

\subsubsection{Neural Network Training}

Three single-hidden-layer networks (L, T, E activations) with architecture [input: Fibonacci sequence length] $\to$ [5 neurons] $\to$ [output: dummy target] were trained using:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Algorithm:} Bayesian regularization (for L, E) or Levenberg--Marquardt (for T)
\item \textbf{Objective:} Mean squared error on dummy task (network weights, not predictions, are the desired output)
\item \textbf{Epochs:} 100--500 depending on convergence
\item \textbf{Overfitting prevention:} Early stopping based on validation set performance
\end{itemize}

The trained networks' $5 \times 5$ weight matrices serve as computational laboratories for trace extraction.

\subsubsection{Elliptic Integral Parameter Regression}

The mapping from (sct, $\sum f_n$, T) $\to$ $m$ was determined via nonlinear least-squares regression on training data, minimizing prediction error for gas composition, yield, and CCE simultaneously. Constraint: $m \in [0, 0.99]$ to ensure elliptic integral convergence.

Final form (empirically determined):
$$m = \tanh\left( \alpha \cdot \text{sct}^{\beta} + \gamma \cdot (\sum f_n)^{\delta} + \epsilon \cdot T^{\zeta} \right)$$

where $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta, \epsilon, \zeta)$ are fitted coefficients.

\subsection{Computational Workflow}

For a new gasification case (specified fuel properties and operating conditions):

\begin{enumerate}
\item Compute CCA variates: $\text{EPS} = [E_1, E_2, S_1, P_1]$
\item Extract geometric features: $(d, K)$ from triangular construction
\item Generate Fibonacci sequence: $\{f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_{15}\}$ using sector-based $N_j$
\item Feed sequence through neural laboratories: Obtain traces, form score vectors $S_{AB}$, $S_{BA}$
\item Construct characteristic matrix: $S_c$ via outer product
\item Measure specific computational time: sct from sorting operation
\item Evaluate elliptic integral: $m \to K(m)$ via AGM ($\sim$5 iterations)
\item Extract transfer function: $(k, \{z_i\}, \{p_j\})$ from ellipap module
\item Compute invariants: $\xi = \ln(\Lambda_S/\Lambda_G)$, $S = |p| - |z|$
\item Predict observables: Yield from $\xi$, CCE from $S$, composition from full $H(s)$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Computational cost:} $\sim$50 milliseconds per case on standard workstation (Intel i7, 16GB RAM), dominated by neural network forward passes. This represents a $\sim$10$^6\times$ speedup relative to full CFD simulation (hours per case on HPC cluster).

